{"entity": "Oscilloscope", "type": "artifact", "description": "An oscilloscope is an electronic test instrument primarily used to display and analyze the waveforms of electrical signals. It visually represents signals over time, allowing users to observe and measure various characteristics such as waveform shape, frequency, and peak-to-peak voltage. The instrument is equipped with controls for channel selection, voltage scale, time base, position, and trigger settings, enabling precise adjustment of the displayed signal. Many oscilloscopes also feature automatic measurement functions and cursor-based measurement tools to facilitate quantitative analysis.\n\nThe oscilloscope typically incorporates vertical, horizontal, and trigger system settings to manage signal acquisition and display. It may include multiple sampling modes and an internal calibration signal to ensure measurement accuracy. Beyond standard signal observation, the oscilloscope is employed to study transient processes and to examine the behavior of differential and integrating circuits through waveform monitoring. Overall, it serves as a versatile tool for debugging, characterization, and analysis of electronic signals in both educational and professional settings.<SEP>An instrument used to observe Lissajous figures for measuring phase differences.<SEP>An instrument used to observe and display waveforms in the experiment.<SEP>An oscilloscope used as teaching equipment in the course.<SEP>An instrument used to observe waveform shapes, specifically to verify the generation of a sine wave.<SEP>An instrument used to observe voltage waveforms at various points in the filter circuit."}
